.TH EZSSH 1 "2026-09-20" "caveutils" "caveutils Manual"
.SH NAME
ezssh \- terminal manager for SSH: hosts, tunnels and keys in one tool,
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ezssh hosts
.br
.B ezssh tunnels
.br
.B ezssh keys
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ezssh
.nf
built on lib/lazytui.sh. It only drives the real ssh, ssh-keygen,
ssh-copy-id, scp/rsync and sshfs, and edits ~/.ssh/config (a dated backup
is written before every change).

  ezssh [hosts | tunnels | keys]

Hosts lists every Host in your ssh config (wildcards left out) with what
ssh will really use for it, plus a live "reachable" marker filled in a
moment after the list appears. Enter connects. Tunnels lists the port
forwards started from here, which run in the background on a control
socket so they can be stopped again. Keys lists ~/.ssh/*.pub with their
fingerprints. Tab / Shift-Tab cycle the three views.

Hosts:   Enter connect, Alt-T open a tunnel (local port forward), Alt-C
         install your key on the host (ssh-copy-id), Alt-F upload a file,
         Alt-G download a file, Alt-M / Alt-U mount / unmount with sshfs
         under ~/mnt, Alt-K forget the host key, Alt-N add a host, Ctrl-D
         remove a host block, Alt-E edit the config.
Tunnels: Ctrl-X stop the marked tunnels.
Keys:    Alt-N generate a key (ed25519), Enter show the public key, Alt-Y
         copy it to the clipboard, Alt-A add it to ssh-agent.

Set EZSSH_CONFIG to use another config file. Anything that needs a tool
you don't have (sshfs, rsync, a clipboard) tells you instead of failing.

.fi
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B hosts
hosts from the ssh config (default)
.TP
.B tunnels
tunnels started by ezssh
.TP
.B keys
your public keys
